<article class="prose-izumi"><p class="eyebrow">Concepts</p><h1>P2P explained</h1><p class="lede">P2P means the video is downloaded from other peers instead of one central streaming server.</p>
<h2>Direct P2P in izumi</h2><p>izumi’s native torrent engine downloads pieces while you watch, buffers ahead, and may upload pieces back to peers. It is temporary playback data, not a permanent library download. Your public IP is visible to the torrent swarm, so use a VPN and its kill switch if that is part of your threat model.</p>
<h2>Why Companion usually needs izumi open</h2><p>The TV does not contain the desktop torrent engine. For a raw P2P source, izumi provides the local torrent session and playback path. A debrid provider changes the shape of the source: it caches the torrent remotely and returns a normal HTTP link that Companion can play.</p>
<aside class="callout"><strong>Watch together note</strong><br />Hosting a watch-together room on debrid shares the host’s resolved account-bound link with guests. Providers may treat multiple guest IPs as account sharing. Prefer each guest’s own account or direct P2P where appropriate.</aside>
<h2>Choosing a mode</h2><ul><li><strong>Prefer debrid:</strong> best fit for a TV-only setup and usually the most reliable start time.</li><li><strong>Direct P2P:</strong> no debrid account, but needs izumi open and exposes the device to the swarm.</li><li><strong>HTTP source:</strong> simplest for Companion when the URL and media format are TV-compatible.</li></ul></article>
